I now have comprehensive understanding of the codebase. Let me compile the full implementation plan.

---

## Implementation Plan: Custom SSE MCP Server Support for Collections

This plan covers adding the ability for users to connect remote MCP servers (via HTTP/SSE URL) to their collections, discover tools, and proxy tool execution. This corresponds to **Phase 1: Remote MCP Import** from the existing `docs/MCP-AGGREGATOR-DESIGN.md`.

---

### 1. Database Schema Changes

**File: `/Users/ajaxdavis/repos/tpmjs/tpmjs/packages/db/prisma/schema.prisma`**

Add two new models, following the exact same pattern as `BridgeConnection` + `CollectionBridgeTool`:

```prisma
/// CustomMcpServer - remote MCP servers registered by a user
model CustomMcpServer {
  id            String    @id @default(cuid())

  // Owner relationship (user-level, not collection-level)
  userId        String    @map("user_id")
  user          User      @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  // Server identity
  name          String    @db.VarChar(100) // User-provided display name, e.g. "My Slack MCP"
  url           String    @db.Text         // The SSE/HTTP endpoint URL

  // Authentication (encrypted, same pattern as envVars)
  authType      String?   @map("auth_type") @db.VarChar(20)  // 'bearer' | 'header' | null
  authHeader    String?   @map("auth_header") @db.VarChar(100) // Header name (default: Authorization)
  authToken     String?   @map("auth_token") @db.Text         // Encrypted token value

  // Cached tool definitions (same pattern as BridgeConnection.tools)
  tools         Json      @default("[]") @db.JsonB
  lastSyncAt    DateTime? @map("last_sync_at")
  lastSyncError String?   @map("last_sync_error") @db.Text
  status        String    @default("pending") @db.VarChar(20) // 'pending' | 'synced' | 'error'

  // Timestamps
  createdAt     DateTime  @default(now()) @map("created_at")
  updatedAt     DateTime  @updatedAt @map("updated_at")

  // Relations
  collectionTools CollectionCustomTool[]

  @@unique([userId, url])
  @@index([userId])
  @@index([status])
  @@map("custom_mcp_servers")
}

/// CollectionCustomTool - tracks which custom server tools are added to collections
model CollectionCustomTool {
  id           String          @id @default(cuid())

  // Collection relationship
  collectionId String          @map("collection_id")
  collection   Collection      @relation(fields: [collectionId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  // Custom server relationship
  serverId     String          @map("server_id")
  server       CustomMcpServer @relation(fields: [serverId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  // Tool reference (from the cached tools JSON)
  toolName     String          @map("tool_name") @db.VarChar(200)

  // Display customization
  displayName  String?         @map("display_name") @db.VarChar(100)
  note         String?         @db.VarChar(500)

  // Timestamps
  createdAt    DateTime        @default(now()) @map("created_at")
  updatedAt    DateTime        @updatedAt @map("updated_at")

  @@unique([collectionId, serverId, toolName])
  @@index([collectionId])
  @@index([serverId])
  @@map("collection_custom_tools")
}
```

Also update the `Collection` model to add the new relation:

```prisma
// In Collection model, add:
customTools CollectionCustomTool[]
```

And update the `User` model:

```prisma
// In User model, add:
customMcpServers CustomMcpServer[]
```

**Rationale**: This mirrors the `BridgeConnection` / `CollectionBridgeTool` pattern exactly. The `CustomMcpServer` is user-level (one server can be added to multiple collections), and `CollectionCustomTool` is the junction table per-collection. Tools are cached as JSON on the server record, just like `BridgeConnection.tools`.

